From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Fri, 29 Jul 94 22:18:38 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-266] SELF: my heart (pumping) 

>ten and fifteen years ago my pulse was usually in the high eighties,
>and now it's in the high sixties.  Why would that be?  I haven't
>started running marathons or anything.  I stopped drinking sugary
>sodas; I've cut way down on red meat, pizza and fatty Chinese food.
>Would any of that do it?

I think some of the studies Roy Walford cites show that both
decreasing percent-calories-from-fat and decreasing total calorie
intake can lead to reduced resting pulse-rate.

This is from memory, though, and I might easily be remembering 
wrong.

My two cents about life-extension techniques and health in general:
after my most recent swing through the literature, which was more
extensive and more careful than any of my previous swings, I've
decided that the evidence demonstrating life-extending effects from a
gradual (five years or so!) reduction in calorie intake and/or a
reduction in percent-calories-from-fat is much more persuasive than
*any* of the evidence demonstrating life-extending effects from any
sort of supplemental nutrients or drugs added to a typical American
diet.

The typical American diet has come down from (historical maximum?) 40%
calories from fat to (currently) 37% calories from fat. The ever
trustworthy Feds assert that 30% calories from fat is ideal. All the
research I've seen tells *me* that 10% to 15% is probably best. I'd
expect that most of the benefits obtained by vegans come from one or
possibly both of two effects: it's easy to eat low fat on a vegan
diet, and it's easy to feel full on fewer calories on a vegan diet.

Roy Walford's principal popular book, with much more extensive details
and pointers into the scientific literature, is called THE 120 YEAR
DIET. As with Harry Browne's best book, we have here another
contestant for "the best book with the worst title" award.

