"Brady, Mark" <mbrady@msmail.his.tch.tmc.edu> said :

> The big issue here is the Federal government wanting to outlaw assault 
> rifles in the latest anti-crime bill.  Used in less than 1% of crimes, they 
> are still high profile political targets.  I don't favor making them illegal 
> for a variety of reasons, the right to privacy comes to mind first (I don't 
> buy the NRA's Citizen's rights to bear arms), but I was wondering what the 
> other opinions on the issue were.

Well, it seems kind-of strange that you're opposed to the gun ban based
on a supposed "right to privacy" that's mentioned nowhere in the US
constitution that I can find (except perhaps under the ninth or tenth
amendments), when the second amendment clearly states that "the right
to keep and bear arms shall not be infringed". However, I'm not an
American or a lawyer, so I suppose there might be some semantic
justification for believing that "the right to keep and bear arms shall
not be infringed" really means that "the right to keep and bear arms
shall be infringed by the government whenever it feels like it, and
shall only apply to guns in any case".

As you say, "assault weapons" (and from what I've seen of the bill,
that basically means "guns that they don't like the look of") are
hardly ever used in crimes (not suprising, since they're mostly
designed for battlefield use where the conditions dictate different
tradeoffs between concealibility and ease of use than would be the case
for a gun designed for holding up liquor stores), and the only possible
justification other than the cosmetic one is fear, most likely fear of
them being used against the government when it passes all the new
police-state legislation that's on its way. Even if the law wasn't
unconstitutional, fear is by far the worst motivator for any kind of
legislation.

It seems to me that the greatest supporters of "gun control" (I prefer
"gun banning" myself, since many governments have banned them, but none
have ever succeeded in controlling them) are people who've never lived
in a disarmed society. What, for example, would you do if you and
everyone nearby are unarmed and some guy starts beating up a passerby
on the street, if someone breaks into your house and decides to beat
you up and rape your wife and children, breaks into your office and
calmly ignores the burglar alarm as they load all the computers into a
van, runs your wife's car off the road and decides to rob or rape her,
etc ? Those aren't hypothetical questions - I currently live in Britain
(you know, that society that's supposedly ultra-safe because of all the
gun laws), and many of these are quite common occurences over here.
Since the government have taken away our ability to defend ourselves,
there's basically nothing to do but call the cops and hope that they
get there in time.

The UK Bill of Rights (1689) says "That the subjects which are
Protestants may have arms for their defence suitable to their
conditions and as allowed by law", which was at least clearer than the
US version, but unfortunately had that wimp-out clause at the end (they
weren't biased against non-Protestants, but one of the complaints that
the bill starts with was that the Catholic king had confiscated arms
from a number of Protestants). That right lasted until 1920, when the
first gun registration law was passed (despite considerable
opposition), supposedly to reduce armed crime, but in fact (from papers
released years later) to ensure that the government knew where all the
guns were in case there was a revolution. It's kind of lucky that the
government were lying, as the annual number of crimes with guns has
gone up by about a factor of 300 in the last seventy-four years.

Since then, despite claims that it wouldn't lead to guns being banned,
every decade or so regulations are tightened and more guns are banned,
and back in the late eighties when "assault rifles" were banned over
here, the chief of the ACPO (Association of Chief Police Officers)
apparently stated in a radio interview that his goal was to see no guns
in the hands of the public. Since that law was passed (which also
dramatically increased the controls on shotguns) the number of armed
robberies with guns has been doubling every three years, and, while
it's still rather lower than the US, at that rate it won't be long
before we catch up. To some extent this is not suprising - aside from
the fact that every few weeks the cops discover another cache of SMGs
and other guns that have been imported from Eastern Europe, I've read
claims that compliance with the new shotgun restrictions was as low as
20%, which would put up to another 200,000 shotguns in the hands of
criminals (and if the government only got a 20% compliance rate in
Britain, how well do you think they'd do in the US, without serious
police-state tactics ?).

In terms of crime rates, I don't know if the number of guns has much
effect on most of them, the per-capita murder rate in Britain is about
1/5-1/4 of the rate in the US, so if you avoid inner cities your odds
of being killed by amateurs (as opposed to the government) in either
country aren't much different, and overall the rate of violent crime is
very similar. Britain has a significantly higher burglary rate than the
US, a significantly higher rape rate, and according to one newspaper
earlier this year, we're ten times as likely to have a car stolen as
someone in the US. Also, a couple of months ago a newspaper article
claimed that the police had decided that after years of telling people
to put more locks on their houses, they now recommended not trying *too*
hard as if you gave the burglar a hard time they were more likely to
kill you or beat you up when they got in. Next, I expect, they'll be
recommending that we serve tea and biscuits while we're robbed.

So anyway, that's a quick precis of my view of life in a disarmed
society. If you want to live in a society where a group of boys can
rape a group of girls on a train with no attempt to stop them, where
the government can pass arbitrary nonsense like the recent Criminal
(In-)Justice Act which appears to make pretty much anyone other than
middle-class conservatives into a criminal, and where expansive
police-state powers are justified on the grounds that the government
(which took away your ability to protect *yourself* in the first place)
needs then in order to protect you, then "gun control" will be a good
way to get there.

Personally, I'm out of here in a few months, and I don't intend to come
back. Were it not for the recent police-state legislation in the US
(including the "assault weapons" bill) I'd be moving over there, but as
it is I'm going to be looking elsewhere.  Anyone know when the Chinese
take over Hong Kong (I know it's 1997, but is that beginning, end, or
some other time ?)

			Mark

